Split out of karr #101 / docs/adr/0021 rather than folded in, so that change stays the one gate the ADR describes.

ADR 0021 fixes the window [2^63 .. 2^64-1] for values that arrive through Format::JSON::parse: the parser hands back the float Go reads, and rotate/encrypt of a sops-written document works again. A value that does NOT come from a JSON parse -- a Perl UV literal, a computed integer, or a scalar out of a YAML parse -- still hits assert_representable and croaks.

Measured (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops, 35 literals across the window, unencrypted JSON slot, guard switched off in a scratchpad patch): 14 of the 35 would have produced a document sops -d accepts, and are refused. The other 21 would fail their own MAC and the refusal is right. In an ENCRYPTED slot and in BOTH YAML slots, all 35 are correct refusals (type:int -> strconv.Atoi out of range, exit 25; YAML -> sops cannot walk uint64, exit 23).

The acceptance condition is the ADR 0006 one, measured with no exceptions in either direction: the literal text equals FormatFloat(double,\x27f\x27,-1,64) of the double it names.

So this is the slot-dependent guard karr #101 sketched, and the reason it is not in ADR 0021 is cost, not doubt: it needs a mac_covered-style flag threaded through Format::JSON::serialize, a reject_scalar hook the JSON emitter does not have today, and the int64 rung dropped from File::SOPS::_compute_mac\x27s slot-blind sweep and re-covered by Encrypted::encrypt_value for encrypted slots. Four files, and it LOOSENS a guard.

Cheap partial fix worth doing on its own: assert_representable\x27s message currently offers only "Pass it as a string to store it exactly". Since ADR 0021 the answer that matches sops is also available -- pass the value as a float -- and the message should say so.

Wire lane. Needs the sops binary to prove. Do not start before ADR 0021 has landed.
VORSCHLAG aus karr #101 Lane 3 (API), 2026-08-20, gemessen aber NICHT umgesetzt -- gehoert in dieses Ticket. Die Meldung in Encrypted.pm assert_representable schliesst heute mit 'Pass it as a string to store it exactly.' Das ist unvollstaendig, seit ADR 0021 lebt: 'pass it as a float' ist inzwischen die Antwort, die zu sops passt, und die Meldung bietet sie nicht an. Gemessen mit einem vom Aufrufer gelieferten unpack('d',pack('d',$lit)), 3 Literale x 2 Formate x 2 Slots: JSON verschluesselt UND unverschluesselt geschrieben, YAML verschluesselt geschrieben, YAML UNVERSCHLUESSELT croakt (ADR 0013) solange die kanonische Dezimale noch unter uint64max liegt -- darueber deklariert _go_int ab und es wird geschrieben. sops -d der JSON-Faelle ist exit 0 mit 9223372036854776000 in beiden Slots. Die Formulierung darf den Float-Weg also NICHT bedingungslos versprechen. Vorgeschlagener Ersatz nur fuer den Schlusssatz: 'Two answers, and which one you want is yours to say: pass it as a string to store the digits exactly (type:str, written verbatim, both formats, both slots), or as a float -- unpack(d, pack(d, $value)) -- to get what sops writes for the same digits, type:float and the double canonical decimal, at the cost of the digits the double cannot hold. A bare JSON number in this range already takes the second answer, because Go own decoder read it as a float64 (karr #101, ADR 0021). The float answer does not reach a YAML UNENCRYPTED slot, which refuses it separately (ADR 0013) unless the canonical decimal is past uint64max.' OFFEN: die Wire-Lane soll die negative Haelfte nachmessen, bevor das uebernommen wird -- ein Integer-SV kann nicht unter int64min, der Fall ist vermutlich unerreichbar, aber Lane 3 hat dieses Ende nicht bewiesen.
